Hi [~accountid:712020:634c40ac-eff2-4487-aaa9-f38b92a7bf83] 

advisor ideas:

{color:#4c9aff}Bolt's payment to the Courier (covering the discount) might be seen as third-party consideration, taxable under VAT Directive Article 73. Sveral times ruled by the ECJ, VAT seeks to tax the recipient’s expenditure on a supply of goods or services obtained by the supplier from the recipient. That amount is extended in Article 73 of the VAT Directive to include expenditure borne by a third party. Accordingly, the consideration obtained by the supplier from a third party “in return for the supply” is also covered.{color}

I don’t think this is a big issue, the courier invoice is already in full with full VAT.

{color:#4c9aff}Bolt cannot deduct the VAT related to the discount portion, as invoices from courier are for the full amount.{color}

Not a blocker.

{color:#4c9aff}For B2B customers, invoicing amounts not fully paid by the Customer could raise concerns about unjustified CIT deductions.{color}

{color:#4c9aff}For B2C customers, this concern is irrelevant.{color}

This has been flagged a couple of times in PT (about coupons for example), but this would be an issue for the customer, not Bolt.

{color:#4c9aff}Concerns about whether granting a subsidy without an underlying invoice could affect deductibility in Bolt's Estonian operations.{color}

Not an issue currently as we run the centralized model and don’t pay CIT in EE. If we localize Food in PT, this can be a major issue as costs without documentation not only are not deductible but also are subject to an autonomous tax of 50%.



All in all, no blockers to proceed.

